# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
BLOCKS = {
    "base": [
        ("Shaping a base that holds its breaks",
         "<p>A putting green's base in {city} is a smaller, more deliberate version of a lawn base, hand-shaped into the designed slopes and tiers rather than screeded flat like the rest of the yard. Washed, open-graded crushed rock or crushed concrete still goes down two to four inches deep in compacted lifts, but each lift gets shaped to the break plan before the next one starts, since correcting a slope after the surface is bonded down is far harder than getting it right in the rock. A green that loses its shape within a season almost always started with a base that was compacted flat and shaped only at the very top layer.</p>"),
        ("Why a contoured green still needs an overall slope",
         "<p>Even with tiers and breaks built into the surface for play, a {city} putting green's base still carries the same one to two percent overall grade toward a drainage point that any lawn does, so the two jobs, holding a shape and shedding water, happen at once rather than one after the other. A green shaped purely for interesting breaks without that underlying slope will hold water in its lowest tier after every storm, regardless of how well the ball rolls the rest of the time. Getting both requirements satisfied in the same base is what separates a green built by someone experienced with the shaping from a flat pad with decoration on top.</p>"),
        ("Why washed rock matters more under a putting surface",
         "<p>The same rule requiring washed rock under any {city} lawn applies to a putting green's base, and the consequences of skipping it show up faster here than almost anywhere else on the property. Fines from unwashed fill binding into a crust don't just slow drainage the way they would under a lawn; they also create an uneven surface underneath the shaped tiers, which shows up as a dead spot or an unexpected bounce exactly where a ball should be rolling true. A green's fine tolerance for surface consistency makes a crusted, uneven base far more noticeable in daily use than the same problem would be under grass nobody's testing with a putter.</p>"),
        ("Letting the sand under a green keep draining",
         "<p>The state's rule against over-compacting the subgrade so tightly that it stops draining applies to a putting green exactly as it does to a lawn, even though a green's base gets shaped and hand-worked far more than a flat lawn base ever does. Native sand under a {city} green still needs to percolate water down and away, since a shaped base sitting on sealed-off sand just holds rainwater in its lowest tier rather than passing it through to the ground below. Contouring the rock layer for playability can't come at the cost of leaving the sand underneath unable to do its job.</p>"),
        ("Why a green's low tiers have to outdrain the storm",
         "<p>Perforated backing on a putting surface passes water at over thirty inches an hour on its own, the same rate any turf backing does, but a {city} green's lowest tier is where that number gets tested hardest during a typical one- to three-inch summer downpour. A base shaped for breaks without matching drainage capacity in its lowest points will pond there first, since water naturally collects in a tier's low corner before it has anywhere else to go. Building that drainage capacity into the base from the start, rather than hoping the backing alone handles it, is what keeps a green playable the morning after a storm instead of soggy until afternoon.</p>"),
    ],
    "product": [
        ("Nylon or textured polyethylene for the putting surface?",
         "<p>A {city} putting green's playing surface is built from either short nylon or textured polyethylene fiber, cut far shorter than any lawn turf so a ball rolls across the tips of the blades rather than settling into them. Nylon holds its shape and stiffness longer under repeated ball contact and foot traffic, while textured polyethylene offers a softer feel underfoot at a lower cost. Neither fiber choice changes the base or drainage requirements underneath; the decision comes down to how firm the roll should feel and how much daily play the surface is expected to take.</p>"),
        ("Why the fringe is a different product than the green",
         "<p>The taller collar surrounding a {city} putting surface, the fringe, is a separate turf product from the short putting surface itself, cut and seamed as its own piece so the transition reads like a mowing line rather than a patch. Fringe turf runs at a pile height closer to a residential lawn blend than to the closely cropped putting surface, giving a chip or a rolled approach shot somewhere realistic to sit before reaching the green. Choosing fringe height and color to contrast just slightly with the surrounding lawn helps define the green's edge without needing a hard border.</p>"),
        ("Cups: the hardware inside a green's design",
         "<p>Cups are cored directly through the finished base at whatever locations the break design calls for, then set and edged so they sit flush with the surrounding putting surface on a {city} green. The number and placement of cups is as much a product decision as the turf fiber itself, since each one interrupts the shaped surface and has to be positioned where the breaks actually make sense to putt toward. Adding cups after the surface is already down means recutting finished turf, which is why cup locations get fixed during the design phase rather than adjusted later.</p>"),
        ("What stimp speed actually depends on",
         "<p>Ball roll speed on a {city} putting green, measured on a stimpmeter, comes from a combination of fiber type, infill, and grooming rather than any single spec on a product sheet, which is why two greens built with the same nylon can still stimp differently. Home installations generally land somewhere in the eight to thirteen range depending on that combination, with nylon and a lighter, more even infill layer typically producing a faster, more consistent roll than a heavier infill or a less groomed surface. Asking for a stimp reading on an installer's own finished work says more than a manufacturer's brochure number ever will.</p>"),
        ("What the putting green price range includes",
         "<p>Backyard putting green installation runs fourteen to thirty dollars a square foot, typically eighteen to twenty-five, well above a plain lawn's range because the base is hand-shaped rather than screeded flat and the surface product itself is chosen for ball roll instead of appearance. A {city} homeowner comparing a lawn quote to a green quote for the same square footage should expect that difference to show up in labor and shaping time as much as in the turf product, since contouring a base to hold breaks takes longer than compacting one flat ever does.</p>"),
    ],
    "process": [
        ("Designing the breaks before any digging starts",
         "<p>Before excavation begins on a {city} green, the slopes and tiers get laid out on paper or staked directly on the ground, since the base has to be built to hold whatever shape the design calls for rather than adjusted after the fact. Skipping this planning step and shaping the base by eye during excavation tends to produce a green that looks fine on handoff day but doesn't play the way the homeowner pictured, since breaks that seemed obvious on a walk-through are harder to judge once dirt starts moving. Every later step, from cup placement to fringe seaming, depends on this layout being settled first.</p>"),
        ("Why a green needs deeper excavation than a lawn",
         "<p>Excavation for a {city} green runs four to six inches, noticeably more than a flat lawn conversion covering the same square footage, because the extra depth makes room for a thicker, hand-shaped base without the finished green ending up perched above the surrounding turf. Too little depth and the shaped layers underneath can't hold their contours once the surface product goes on top; too much and the green sits in a shallow bowl that collects runoff from the lawn around it instead of shedding its own water outward. Confirming that number against the design before the first shovel of soil comes out saves a mid-project correction.</p>"),
        ("Seaming the putting surface and fringe as separate pieces",
         "<p>The short putting surface and the taller fringe turf around a {city} green get cut and seamed as two distinct pieces rather than one continuous roll, since they're different products at different pile heights doing different jobs. That seam is placed deliberately along the design's edge line so it reads as a mowing transition once grooming is finished, not as a visible patch where two products meet. Getting this seam line right the first time matters more on a green than on a lawn, since the edge is exactly where a chip shot lands and a rough transition is the most visible spot on the entire surface.</p>"),
        ("Setting cups once the base is fully shaped",
         "<p>Cups go in after the {city} green's base has been shaped, compacted, and confirmed to hold its breaks, cored through at the design's marked locations rather than positioned by eye during installation. Each cup gets set and edged individually, which takes longer per hole than any other single step in building a green, since a slightly crooked or poorly seated cup stands out immediately against a surface built for precision. Waiting until the base is finished before coring also avoids having to patch a cup location that turns out to sit in the wrong spot once the shape is complete.</p>"),
        ("Infill and a roll test before calling the job done",
         "<p>The last step on a {city} green is a thin, even layer of infill followed by a roll test, checking that a ball tracks true across the surface rather than pulling toward one side from uneven grooming or nap direction. Adjustments at this stage are usually small, a pass with the brush in a different direction, or a bit more infill in one section, rather than anything structural, since the base and surface work is already finished by this point. Skipping the roll test and assuming a green plays correctly because it looks finished is how a pulling break goes unnoticed until the first real game.</p>"),
    ],
    "risks": [
        ("How summer heat changes a green's roll",
         "<p>A {city} putting surface in full sun reaches the same 120 to 150°F range as any other turf, uncomfortable enough for bare feet that most play happens in shoes even at home, and that heat can make a nylon surface feel marginally faster as the fiber stiffens in extreme temperatures. A hose rinse drops that surface temperature 30 to 50 degrees within minutes, which cools the green for comfortable play but also softens the roll slightly right afterward until the surface dries. Timing a rinse well before a round, rather than right before stepping onto the green, avoids that temporary change in speed.</p>"),
        ("Low-E glass near a pool-cage green",
         "<p>Greens built inside or beside a screened pool cage are common in {city}, and a west-facing sliding door or low-emissivity window nearby is worth checking before finalizing the layout, since reflected sunlight from that kind of glass can soften turf enough to damage it. The fringe around a green, made from polyethylene that starts to give around 175 to 200°F, is more exposed to this risk than the nylon putting surface itself, which tolerates heat somewhat better. A scorched patch along one edge of an otherwise undamaged green usually traces back to exactly this kind of reflection.</p>"),
        ("Drip lines and a shaded green under mature oaks",
         "<p>The same drip-line rule that keeps turf away from a live oak's roots applies to a putting green, and a shaded spot under mature canopy that looks appealing for a {city} green during the hottest months still requires a certified arborist's sign-off before any shaping or excavation happens nearby. Excavating four to six inches for a green's base is deeper than a lawn conversion, which makes root damage a bigger risk under canopy than it would be for grass alone. Choosing a different, sunnier spot for the green and keeping the shaded area as fringe or lawn instead is often the simpler path.</p>"),
        ("Setbacks for a green on a golf-community pond lot",
         "<p>A {city} lot backing onto a pond or canal, common in golf-course communities, still has to keep a putting green at least ten feet from the water's edge unless a seawall separates the two, and the green can't be shaped to route drainage through a swale even where that would be the most convenient direction to send water. These setbacks sometimes conflict with where a homeowner pictures the green relative to the view, which is why confirming the buildable area against the property's actual waterline comes before any design work on breaks and tiers.</p>"),
        ("When a base is shaped for looks instead of drainage",
         "<p>A {city} green built by someone chasing dramatic breaks without matching drainage capacity in the low points will look impressive on a dry day and pond badly after the first real storm, since water collects in exactly the tiers designed to be most visually interesting. Standing water sitting in a low tier softens that part of the base well before the rest of the green shows any wear, and the surface above it stops rolling the way it was designed to soon after, sometimes within a single rainy season. Shaping decisions and drainage planning have to happen together from the start, not as a layer added on top of an afterthought base.</p>"),
    ],
    "care": [
        ("Brushing a green to keep the roll consistent",
         "<p>Consistency in a {city} green's roll comes far more from regular grooming than from the age of the surface itself, which is why a light pass with a stiff brush, moving opposite the nap direction, belongs on a repeating schedule rather than a once-a-year task. Skipping several months between brushings lets the fiber settle into whatever direction foot traffic and rain have pushed it, and a ball crossing that section starts to pull slightly off line even though nothing about the green looks different to the eye. A gentle, frequent touch keeps the surface predictable in a way that one thorough grooming a year never quite manages.</p>"),
        ("Clearing debris from a green's low tiers",
         "<p>Leaves and pine needles collect fastest in a {city} green's low tiers, the same spots already doing the most drainage work, and letting debris sit there breaks it down into the infill and can slow water movement exactly where the base needs to move it fastest. Clearing those low spots promptly after they accumulate, rather than waiting for a general yard cleanup, keeps both the roll and the drainage performing as designed. A green with several tiers takes a few extra minutes to check compared with a flat lawn, since debris doesn't distribute evenly across a shaped surface.</p>"),
        ("Topping off infill on a putting surface",
         "<p>A {city} green's infill thins gradually with every rinse, rainstorm, and round played on it, and because the putting surface is groomed to a tighter tolerance than a lawn, that gradual loss shows up in how a ball rolls before it's ever visible to the eye. Adding a light layer back in every year or two, rather than waiting for a visibly bare patch to appear, keeps the roll behaving the way it did on day one instead of drifting toward faster or bouncier as the months add up. Checking depth on a set schedule beats waiting for a complaint about a mis-hit putt to prompt the work.</p>"),
        ("Checking cups after a storm",
         "<p>Cups set into a {city} green's base can work loose after heavy rain or wind, especially if water pooled around one during a storm and softened the surrounding base slightly. A quick check after any significant weather, confirming each cup still sits flush and hasn't shifted, catches this before a loose cup becomes an obvious problem during play. Reseating a cup that's started to work loose is a small fix if caught early, compared with the surrounding turf needing repair if it's left to worsen over multiple storms.</p>"),
        ("Rinsing a green for heat, dust, and grooming",
         "<p>A hose rinse on a {city} green does double duty: it drops surface temperature 30 to 50 degrees for more comfortable play on a hot afternoon, and it clears dust and pollen that would otherwise interfere with a clean, consistent roll. Rinsing before a grooming pass, rather than after, also helps the brush move infill more evenly, since damp infill settles differently than dry infill does under a stiff brush. This small sequencing choice, rinse first, then groom, makes routine maintenance slightly more effective without adding any extra steps to the routine itself.</p>"),
    ],
}
